  "metadata": {
    "title": "The Kissing Bridge",
    "description": "A covered bridge over a river, wide enough for the hay wagon. Barn-red walls and a hipped roof enclose the span over the water. That's the old way of making a timber bridge last: the roof keeps the rain off the deck. Courting couples were said to steal a kiss in the dark inside, which is why covered bridges were nicknamed kissing bridges. 31 bricks.\n\n## How it is built\n\nEach step is a group in the Editor, so you can select it, delete it or build it again yourself.\n\n1. **River**: four blue Slab 4x4s make a river 8 wide.\n2. **Deck**: six wooden Plate 2x4s side by side, 12 long and 4 wide.\n3. **Walls**: three barn-red Wall 2x3s along each edge of the deck, enclosing the 6 in the middle.\n4. **Roof**: one Hip Roof 4x6 on top of the walls, turned 90° (press **R**) so its long side runs along the road.\n5. **Rails**: a wooden Fence on each side of the deck between each end of the covered span and the bank.\n6. **Ramps**: four Shallow Slopes on each bank lead from the ground up to the deck. On the east side they are turned 180° so they climb toward the bridge.\n7. **Lanterns**: one at each end of the deck, on opposite sides. In World View they light up at night.\n\n## Wide enough for the hay wagon\n\nThe hay wagon is about 2 long and 1.4 wide over its wheels, and it takes up a circle 1.6 across. Inside the covered span the road is 3.5 wide between the walls, which leaves almost 1 to spare on each side. The roof starts 3 above the deck, more than twice the height of a loaded wagon.\n\nThe top of each ramp is exactly level with the deck, so a wagon rolls on without catching an edge. The walls and rails run the whole way over the water, so a wagon can't drive off into the river.\n\n## Bricks\n\n- River: 4 Slab 4x4\n- Deck: 6 Plate 2x4\n- Walls: 6 Wall 2x3\n- Roof: 1 Hip Roof 4x6\n- Rails: 4 Fence\n- Ramps: 8 Shallow Slope 2x1\n- Lanterns: 2 Lantern\n\n31 bricks in all.",
